WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.520
From the Hollywood heavy-hitters to the groundbreaking
innovators to the stunning dark horse surprises,

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.300
the early days of 2018 saw no small number
of fantastic films hit the box office.

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:14.050
Luckily for all the cinephiles out there,
it seems like the summer which is traditionally

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:18.330
a benchmark season for studios to release
big pictures has managed to more than live

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.000
up to the hype.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.120
These are a few of the season’s very best
movies.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.240
Hereditary

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.390
Hereditary drew enormous buzz in the months
leading up to its release.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.000
Its debut at Sundance in January 2018 was
greeted with overwhelming praise, with much

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.320
of it lavished on the performance of Toni
Collette.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.800
And while many festival favorites struggle
to find their place among general audiences,

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.860
Hereditary followed through with ease.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:47.540
This audacious feature debut from writer/director
Ari Aster shaped up to be a significant box

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.720
office success, supplemented by an array of
shining reviews.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.600
The general consensus among critics has been
that Collette has delivered the performance

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:55.620
of her career.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.020
Meanwhile, the film’s underlying themes
of menacing family dynamics and the passing

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:05.020
down of tragedy through the generations helps
Hereditary get under your skin and stay there.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.280
New Yorker critic Anthony Lane warns:

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.659
"I would hesitate to recommend it to the readily
traumatized [...] for viewers recuperating

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:16.330
from a wounded childhood, or from a festering
relationship, it could scrape too close to

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:16.820
the bone."

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.100
Ant-Man and the Wasp

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.240
No surprise here: with Ant-Man and the Wasp,
Marvel Studios landed their third smash hit

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.760
of the year and their 20th straight film to
debut at No. 1.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.299
Audiences were in desperate need of a palate
cleanser after Avengers: Infinity War, and

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:37.069
returning director Peyton Reed gave them one
as well as a worthy sequel to 2015's Ant-Man

00:01:37.069 --> 00:01:41.520
that's fleeter on its feet, more assured,
and perhaps even funnier than the original.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.020
"hiya champ Hi was school today?

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:44.720
HAHA"

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.320
Filmfare.com went so far as to call it "the
funniest Marvel film you'll see," which is

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.700
high praise indeed considering that Thor:
Ragnarok was released less than a year earlier.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:58.680
The film’s tone is loose and playful, while
the action sequences are confidently and competently

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.280
staged.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.640
The stakes are suitably low enough, too, making
Ant-Man and the Wasp a much-needed breath

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.980
of fresh air after the sheer trauma that was
Infinity War.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.520
Mission: Impossible - Fallout

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.740
For over two decades, Tom Cruise has put himself
in mortal danger for the amusement of audiences

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:19.590
via the surprisingly resilient Mission: Impossible
franchise, which has failed to lose even an

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.640
iota of momentum despite its star's advancing
age.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.040
Now in his 50s, Cruise continues to insist
on doing every stunt that it's physically

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.400
possible for him to do, which is no short
order for a series which has long favored

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.220
top-shelf action set pieces over plot and
characterization.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:47.760
Director Christopher McQuarrie became the
series' first returning director, remaining

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.100
in the seat previously held by directors like
Brad Bird, John Woo and J.J. Abrams.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.000
His steady hand behind the camera, as well
as Cruise's otherworldly dedication to the

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:01.170
role of eternally put-upon IMF agent Ethan
Hunt, resulted in a crackerjack picture which

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.040
pulled in some of the best reviews of the
entire series.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.960
As impossible as it might seem, this venerable
action series and its star just seem to keep

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.220
getting better with age or, as the Detroit
News put it:

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.220
"Bless this franchise, we're truly not worthy."

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:17.460
BlacKkKLansman

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.120
With a new generation of stunningly talented
African-American filmmakers finally getting

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.720
their place in the spotlight, it's easy to
forget Spike Lee.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:30.060
Nearly 30 years after detonating a cinematic
nuclear bomb with 1989's Do the Right Thing,

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:35.300
Lee has returned to form with BlacKkKlansman
a hilarious, scathing, insightful piece that

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.010
critics have praised near-uniformly as his
best picture in years.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:43.720
John David Washington stars as a black police
detective in the 1970s who with the aid of

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:48.600
a Jewish fellow officer played by Adam Driver
tries to infiltrate the Ku Klux Klan.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.060
Critics were roundly amazed by Lee's mastery
of tone, as he imbues dramatic, high-stakes

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.340
situations with his singular sensibility.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.740
Digital Spy's Hugh Armitage summed up this
consensus neatly, calling the film:

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:05.480
"a rollercoaster that is laugh-out-loud when
it isn't shocking you into stunned silence

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:10.100
[...] a damn fine and funny piece of entertainment,
and a testament to Lee's skills as an artist."

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.560
Crazy Rich Asians

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:17.880
Directed by John M. Chu, Crazy Rich Asians
features Constance Wu leading a nearly all-Asian

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:21.909
cast to tell the story of a native New Yorker
who accompanies her boyfriend to a wedding

00:04:21.909 --> 00:04:22.640
in Singapore.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.200
Soon after release, it became a surprise critical
smash with all the trappings of a genuine

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.400
cultural phenomenon.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:32.680
Reviewer Rendy Jones encapsulated the critics’
consensus by concluding that:

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:38.160
"Crazy Rich Asians takes familiar tropes and
revitalizes them and its entire genre [...] a

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.080
great cast, amazing direction, and powerful
writing."

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.680
“Woo!”

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.639
Like Black Panther before it, Crazy Rich Asians
constitutes a clear message to Hollywood execs

00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:57.680
that heartfelt, well-executed stories featuring
people of color can not only cross cultural

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.270
boundaries, but smash them and earn huge box
office returns in the process.

